# Template Method

Write the fixed sequence of steps once, in a base class, and let each case fill in only the steps that differ.

## Use it when

- several cases follow the same sequence of steps, and only some steps change from case to case;
- a change to the sequence itself (a new first step, a new check at the end) should apply to every case at once;
- new cases following the same sequence are expected.

## Do not use it when

- there is no shared sequence: each case is a different algorithm (that is Strategy);
- there are two cases and the shared part is a couple of lines;
- the only shared thing is a formatting helper: a utility function is enough, and it is not a Template Method.

## Shape

```java
abstract class BoardGame {
    // the template method: the sequence lives here and nowhere else
    public final Result play(List<Player> players) {
        Board board = setUp(players);
        while (!isOver(board)) {
            playTurn(board, nextPlayer(board, players));
        }
        return decideWinner(board);
    }

    protected abstract Board setUp(List<Player> players);
    protected abstract boolean isOver(Board board);
    protected abstract void playTurn(Board board, Player player);
    protected abstract Result decideWinner(Board board);

    protected Player nextPlayer(Board board, List<Player> players) { /* default rotation; a hook */ }
}

final class Checkers extends BoardGame { /* only the four steps */ }
final class Reversi extends BoardGame { /* only the four steps */ }
```

The same idea works with composition: one generic runner that receives the varying steps as objects or functions
(`new GameRunner(setUp, isOver, playTurn, decideWinner)`), as long as every case goes through that runner.

## Done right when

- the sequence is written once, in the template method (marked `final` when the language allows);
- each case implements only the steps that vary, and none of them repeats the sequence;
- every case uses the skeleton, including the cases that existed before it was introduced;
- a change to the sequence is one edit, in the template method.

## Usual mistakes

- A common interface with one method (`run()`), where each implementation still writes the whole sequence: the
  cases look uniform from outside, but the skeleton is copied inside each one.
- A shared helper that formats the output, while each case still calls the steps in order by itself.
- The new cases use the skeleton and the old ones stay as they were.
- Steps so coarse that one "step" contains almost the whole sequence.

## Neighbors

- **Strategy**: the whole rule varies, there is no common skeleton.
- **Factory Method**: often appears as one of the steps of a template method ("create the piece").
# State

Represent each state of an object as its own class, so the object behaves differently as its state changes, without
`switch` statements on the state in every method.

## Use it when

- one object moves through states over time, and several of its operations behave differently in each state;
- the transitions themselves carry rules (what is allowed from where, what happens on entering a state);
- adding a state today would mean editing many methods that switch on the current state.

## Do not use it when

- the "state" is just a status field that is read, with one or two simple checks: an enum and a guard clause are
  clearer;
- the allowed transitions fit in a small table (from, event, to) and the behavior does not differ per state;
- the variant is chosen once and never changes: that is Strategy.

## Shape

```java
interface MachineState {
    MachineState insertCoin(VendingMachine m, int cents);
    MachineState selectItem(VendingMachine m, String code);
}

final class Idle implements MachineState { /* insertCoin -> HasCredit; selectItem -> refuse */ }
final class HasCredit implements MachineState { /* selectItem -> Dispensing or refuse */ }
final class Dispensing implements MachineState { /* everything refused until done */ }

final class VendingMachine {
    private MachineState state = new Idle();
    void insertCoin(int cents) { state = state.insertCoin(this, cents); }
    void selectItem(String code) { state = state.selectItem(this, code); }
}
```

## Done right when

- each state class decides what its operations do and which state comes next;
- the context object only delegates to its current state;
- adding a state means one new class and the transitions that lead to it.

## Usual mistakes

- State classes exist, but the context still checks `if (state instanceof …)` before delegating.
- Most methods in most states only throw "not allowed": a transition table would have been simpler.
- Using State for something that never changes during the object's life.

## Neighbors

- **Strategy**: chosen from outside, stays fixed. State is changed by the object itself.
- **A transition table**: enough when states differ only in which transitions are allowed.
# Strategy

Put each variant of a rule in its own object behind one interface, so the code that uses the rule never names the
variants.

## Use it when

- several variants compute or decide the same thing in different ways (a route, a score, a compression, a sort order);
- the variant is chosen per use (by a field, a request, a setting) and does not change on its own afterwards;
- new variants are expected, and today each one would add a branch in more than one place.

## Do not use it when

- the variants differ only in data (a rate, a limit): keep the data in a table or an enum field;
- there are two variants and nothing suggests a third;
- the variants share a fixed sequence of steps and differ inside it: that is Template Method.

## Shape

```java
interface RoutePlanner {
    Route plan(Point from, Point to, MapData map);   // everything a variant needs comes in here
}

final class FastestRoute implements RoutePlanner { public Route plan(Point a, Point b, MapData m) { /* … */ } }
final class ShortestRoute implements RoutePlanner { public Route plan(Point a, Point b, MapData m) { /* … */ } }
final class ScenicRoute implements RoutePlanner { public Route plan(Point a, Point b, MapData m) { /* … */ } }

final class RoutePlanners {                          // the one place that knows the variants
    private final Map<String, RoutePlanner> byName = Map.of(
        "fastest", new FastestRoute(), "shortest", new ShortestRoute(), "scenic", new ScenicRoute());
    RoutePlanner named(String name) { /* look up, reject unknown names */ }
}

// the client only talks to the interface
Route route = planners.named(request.mode()).plan(request.from(), request.to(), map);
```

An `enum` whose constants each carry their own implementation (an abstract method overridden per constant, or a
lambda passed to the constructor) is the same pattern. A `switch` that only picks the implementation is fine; a
`switch` that holds the behavior is not.

## Done right when

- every variant, including the ones that existed before the new ones, is an implementation of the interface;
- the rule of each variant (its calculation, its validation, its exceptions) lives inside that variant;
- the client calls the interface and never checks which variant it holds;
- adding a variant means one new class and one registration.

## Usual mistakes

- Only the new variants become classes, and the old ones stay in a `switch` next to them.
- The interface is too narrow for one variant, so the client special-cases it (`if (planner instanceof ScenicRoute)`)
  to pass extra data. Widen what the variants receive instead.
- Each variant delegates back to a shared helper that still switches on the variant.
- An enum gains methods that `switch (this)`: the branching moved, it did not go away.

## Neighbors

- **State**: the object changes its own variant over time. Strategy is chosen from outside and stays.
- **Template Method**: the variants share a skeleton and differ in some steps.
- **Command**: packages a request to run later; Strategy packages a rule to run now.

